    "analysis": "<p>Apache Spark's shuffle mechanism is a critical, yet resource-intensive, operation involving data transfer across the network and disk. During a shuffle, intermediate data generated by map tasks is partitioned and written to local disk, then fetched by reduce tasks running on different executors. A <code>Shuffle FetchFailed</code> exception indicates that a reduce task failed to retrieve necessary shuffle data from a peer executor or the external shuffle service. This can be caused by various factors, including network issues, executor crashes, or the shuffle data becoming unavailable due to disk failures or eviction.</p><p>Memory spill Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors, on the other hand, occur when Spark tasks exceed their allocated memory, forcing data to be spilled to disk. While spilling is a normal part of Spark's memory management, excessive or large spills can lead to OOMs if the disk I/O cannot keep up, or if the process still exceeds its maximum heap size even after spilling. These two issues are often intertwined: a <code>FetchFailed</code> can be a symptom of an executor crashing due to an OOM during a shuffle write, making its shuffle data inaccessible. Conversely, poor shuffle performance can exacerbate memory pressure, leading to spills and potential OOMs.</p>",
    "root_cause": "<code>Shuffle FetchFailed</code>: Network instability (packet loss, high latency), executor failures (due to OOMs, JVM crashes), insufficient disk space on shuffle service nodes, external shuffle service not running or misconfigured, data corruption. <code>Memory Spill OOM</code>: Insufficient executor memory (<code>spark.executor.memory</code>), data skew (one task processing significantly more data), inefficient operations (e.g., large joins, aggregations on high-cardinality keys), improper partitioning, JVM garbage collection issues, excessively large records.",
    "bad_code": "```scala\nimport org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession\n\nval spark = SparkSession.builder()\n  .appName(\"ProblematicSparkJob\")\n  .master(\"yarn\")\n  .config(\"spark.executor.memory\", \"4g\") // Default or insufficient memory\n  .config(\"spark.executor.cores\", \"1\")   // Single core, limits parallelism\n  .config(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\", \"200\") // Default, might be too low or high\n  .config(\"spark.shuffle.service.enabled\", \"false\") // Disabling external shuffle service\n  .config(\"spark.network.timeout\", \"120s\") // Short timeout for large shuffles\n  .getOrCreate()\n\n// Imagine a large dataset with significant data skew\nval largeDF = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/to/very/large/skewed/data\")\n\n// Perform a wide transformation (e.g., join, aggregation) on a skewed key\nval resultDF = largeDF.groupBy(\"skewed_key\").count()\n\nresultDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"/path/to/output\")\n\nspark.stop()\n```",
    "solution_desc": "Resolving these issues requires a multi-pronged approach focused on resource allocation, configuration tuning, and data optimization. Increase <code>spark.executor.memory</code> and <code>spark.executor.cores</code> if resources allow, ensuring enough memory for both execution and storage. Enable and properly configure the external shuffle service (<code>spark.shuffle.service.enabled=true</code>) to offload shuffle data serving from executors, improving stability and reducing executor OOMs. Tune <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> to match the cluster's parallelism and data volume, typically aiming for 2-4 tasks per CPU core. Address data skew using techniques like salting for skewed join keys or applying `repartition()` with more partitions on skewed dataframes. Increase network timeouts (<code>spark.network.timeout</code>) for clusters with high latency or large data transfers. Monitor Spark UI to identify bottleneck stages, problematic tasks, and memory usage. Implement smaller batch sizes, use more efficient data formats (Parquet, ORC), and consider JVM garbage collector tuning (e.g., G1GC) for large heaps.",
    "good_code": "```scala\nimport org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession\nimport org.apache.spark.sql.functions._\n\nval spark = SparkSession.builder()\n  .appName(\"OptimizedSparkJob\")\n  .master(\"yarn\")\n  .config(\"spark.executor.memory\", \"8g\")   // Increased executor memory\n  .config(\"spark.executor.cores\", \"4\")     // More cores per executor\n  .config(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\", \"1000\") // Increased partitions for large data\n  .config(\"spark.shuffle.service.enabled\", \"true\") // Enabled external shuffle service\n  .config(\"spark.network.timeout\", \"600s\") // Extended network timeout\n  .config(\"spark.shuffle.io.preferDirectBufs\", \"false\") // Sometimes helps with GC pressure\n  .config(\"spark.memory.fraction\", \"0.6\") // Adjust memory fraction for execution/storage\n  .config(\"spark.memory.storageFraction\", \"0.3\") // Dedicate more memory to execution if needed\n  .config(\"spark.executor.extraJavaOptions\", \"-XX:+UseG1GC -XX:InitiatingHeapOccupancyPercent=35\") // G1GC tuning\n  .getOrCreate()\n\nval largeDF = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/to/very/large/skewed/data\")\n\n// Identify skewed key if possible and repartition/salt\n// Example for salting (conceptual - actual implementation might vary based on skew):\n// val saltedDF = largeDF.withColumn(\"salted_key\", concat(col(\"skewed_key\"), lit(\"_\"), round(rand() * 10)))\n// val resultDF = saltedDF.groupBy(\"salted_key\").count()\n// If not salting, ensure enough partitions to handle the skew.\n\n// Re-partition before a wide transformation if data is known to be skewed and can benefit from it\nval repartitionedDF = largeDF.repartition(col(\"some_column\")) // Or directly repartition by more buckets\n\nval resultDF = repartitionedDF.groupBy(\"skewed_key\").count() // Example of transformation\n\nresultDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"/path/to/output\")\n\nspark.stop()\n```",
    "verification": "<p>Monitor the Spark UI thoroughly, paying close attention to the Stages and Executors tabs. Look for stages with high shuffle write/read bytes, long task durations, and tasks that fail repeatedly. Check the 'Storage' tab to see how much memory each executor is using and if there's significant spilling. Examine executor logs for `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError` or specific shuffle-related errors. If the external shuffle service is enabled, verify its logs for issues. Observe cluster resource utilization (CPU, memory, disk I/O, network) to identify bottlenecks. Successful verification means job completion without `FetchFailed` exceptions or OOMs, reduced shuffle spill to disk, and improved overall job execution time and stability, often reflected in a smoother, more consistent task completion graph in the Spark UI.</p>",
